Shortcut Trick
In any quadrilateral, the angle formed by the bisectors of two adjacent angles is equal to half the sum of the other two remaining angles.

&ang;TES = (&ang;Q + &ang;R) &divide; 2

&ang;TES = (52&deg; + 44&deg;) &divide; 2

&ang;TES = 96&deg; &divide; 2 = 48&deg;

**&there4; The correct answer is 48&deg;.**

**Alternate Method**

**Given:**

In quadrilateral QRST:

&ang;Q = 52&deg;

&ang;R = 44&deg;

The bisectors of &ang;S and &ang;T meet at E.

**Formula Used:**

The sum of all interior angles of a quadrilateral is 360&deg;.

The sum of all interior angles of a triangle is 180&deg;.

**Calculations:**

In quadrilateral QRST:

&rArr; &ang;Q + &ang;R + &ang;S + &ang;T = 360&deg;

&rArr; 52&deg; + 44&deg; + &ang;S + &ang;T = 360&deg;

&rArr; 96&deg; + &ang;S + &ang;T = 360&deg;

&rArr; &ang;S + &ang;T = 360&deg; &minus; 96&deg; = 264&deg;

Dividing both sides by 2:

&rArr; (&ang;S + &ang;T) &divide; 2 = 264&deg; &divide; 2 = 132&deg;

&rArr; &ang;S/2 + &ang;T/2 = 132&deg;

Since SE and TE are the angle bisectors of &ang;S and &ang;T respectively:

&rArr; &ang;EST = &ang;S/2 and &ang;ETS = &ang;T/2

&rArr; &ang;EST + &ang;ETS = 132&deg;

In triangle TES:

&rArr; &ang;TES + &ang;EST + &ang;ETS = 180&deg;

&rArr; &ang;TES + 132&deg; = 180&deg;

&rArr; &ang;TES = 180&deg; &minus; 132&deg; = 48&deg;

**&there4; The correct answer is 48&deg;.**

**Additional Information**

Angle Bisector Theorem in Quadrilaterals

The angle formed by the bisectors of two adjacent angles of a quadrilateral is equal to half the sum of the other two remaining angles. For adjacent angles A and B, the angle of intersection of their bisectors is (&ang;C + &ang;D) &divide; 2.

Sum of Interior Angles

For any n-sided polygon, the sum of interior angles is given by (n &minus; 2) × 180&deg;. For a quadrilateral (n = 4), the sum is always 360&deg;.

Opposite Angle Bisectors

The angle between the bisectors of two opposite angles of a quadrilateral is equal to half the difference of the other two angles.

